`firstName` / `lastName` are inherited from `Person` (`getLastName()`, `getFirstName()` used throughout).

There is a `@PrePersist defaultRegistrationDate()` (lines 162-167) that sets `registrationDate = LocalDate.now()` when unset — this is what makes `membershipNumber` non-null after save.

### Schema — `customer_code` column length (important if the format grows)
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/db/hsqldb/schema.sql:47` → `customer_code VARCHAR(20),`
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/db/h2/schema.sql:40` → `customer_code VARCHAR(20),`
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/db/mysql/schema.sql:37` → `customer_code VARCHAR(20),`
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/db/postgres/schema.sql:35` → `customer_code TEXT,`

The new `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` is 12 chars, so it fits VARCHAR(20).

---

## 4. Service / repository / controller layers

### Service
- Interface: **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicService.java`** — `void saveOwner(Owner)` at line 59, plus `existsOwnerByTelephone`, `existsOwnerByLastNameAndAddress`, `shareHousehold`. No city-count method exists.
- Impl: **`ClinicServiceImpl.java`** — `saveOwner` (238-249), `countNamesakes` (256-260, uses `findByLastNameIgnoreCase` + stream filter), `shareHousehold` (280-293), `householdMembers` (299-305). Related helper: **`HouseholdIdGenerator.java`** (SHA-256 based `HH-...` id — a good pattern reference for deterministic derived values).

### Repository (interface + 3 profile-specific impls)
Interface **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/OwnerRepository.java`**. Relevant methods:
- `long count()` (line 92) — **global** count, used for the sequence.
- `Collection<Owner> findByLastNameIgnoreCase(String)` (line 76).
- `Collection<Owner> findByLastName(String)` / paged, `findById`, `existsByTelephone`, `findAll`, `save`, `delete`.
- **There is NO `countByCity` / per-city query anywhere.** If the new format needs a per-city sequence, you must add one. Existing count query examples to mirror:

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/springdatajpa/SpringDataOwnerRepository.java` (lines 49-64):
```java
@Override
@Query(value = "SELECT owner FROM Owner owner",
       countQuery = "SELECT COUNT(owner) FROM Owner owner")
Page<Owner> findAll(Pageable pageable);

@Override
@Query("SELECT COUNT(owner) FROM Owner owner")
long count();
```
A per-city variant would be e.g. `@Query("SELECT COUNT(owner) FROM Owner owner WHERE LOWER(owner.city) = LOWER(:city)")`.

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/jpa/JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl.java` — `count()` (141-145) `SELECT COUNT(owner) FROM Owner owner`; `findByLastNameIgnoreCase` (100-105) uses `LOWER(owner.lastName) = LOWER(:lastName)`.

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/jdbc/JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl.java` — `count()` (261-267) `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM owners`; note the JDBC SELECTs (lines 86, 101, 125, 168, 211, 226) only project `id, first_name, last_name, address, city, telephone` (they do NOT read `customer_code`), and `findByLastNameIgnoreCase` (164-168) uses `LOWER(last_name)=LOWER(:lastName)`.

Which impl is active depends on the Spring profile (`spring-data-jpa`, `jpa`, `jdbc`). The acceptance tests run the default `@SpringBootTest`.

### Controller
POST/GET handler: **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** — `@RequestMapping("/api")`, `addOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` at 117-152. It maps the DTO to `Owner` via `ownerMapper.toOwner(...)`, normalizes address/telephone/email, does duplicate/household checks, then calls `clinicService.saveOwner(owner)` and returns `ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner)`. `city` flows in from the DTO through the mapper (see below). (There is also `OwnerRestControllerV2.java` at `@RequestMapping("/api")`, v2 package.)

### Mapper
**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** — MapStruct. Line 28: `@Mapping(target = "customerCode", ignore = true)` on `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` (so the client cannot set it; it is server-generated). `toOwnerDto` exposes the computed fields (including `customerCode`, `membershipNumber`, `membershipTier`) via the generated `OwnerDto`.

### DTO / API contract
The `OwnerDto` / `OwnerFieldsDto` are **OpenAPI-generated** from **`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** (into `target/generated-sources`, not checked in). The `customerCode` schema and its description live at lines 2107-2115:

```yaml
customerCode:
  title: Customer code
  description: >-
    The owner's customer code, formatted '<LAST3>-<NNNN>' where LAST3 is
    the upper-cased first three letters of the last name and NNNN is a
    global 4-digit zero-padded sequence assigned at registration.
  type: string
  example: 'FRA-0001'
  readOnly: true
```
`membershipNumber` (2137-2144) and `membershipTier` (2145-2152) descriptions are also here. If you change the format, update this description/example too (it is documentation, not enforced, but the acceptance test regex is the real gate).

---

## 5. Tests referencing customerCode / membership / the format

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java`** — the target spec:

```java
/** customer-code-city: Change the customerCode format to '<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>' where CITY3 is the upper-cased... */
class AcceptanceTest extends AcceptanceBase {

	@Test
	void coreCustomerCodeHasCityPrefix() throws Exception {
		int id = createOwnerOk(ownerNode());
		JsonNode n = fetchOwner(id);
		assertTrue(n.get("customerCode").asText().matches("[A-Z]{3}-[A-Z]{3}-\\d{4}")); // <CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>
	}
}
```

So the expected new format is `[A-Z]{3}-[A-Z]{3}-\d{4}` = `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`, where CITY3 is the upper-cased first three letters of the **city**.

**`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java`** — the harness. Key details for editing/generating test data:
- `ownerNode()` (117-125) builds a fully-unique owner with `city = "Town" + seq()` (via `uniqueCity()`, line 94), `lastName = "Sur" + letters(seq())`. So CITY3 would be `TOW` and LAST3 `SUR` for the default node.
- Tests are `@Transactional` (rolled back), `@WithMockUser` admin roles, hitting `POST /api/owners` and `GET /api/owners/{id}`.
- `fillCity(String city, int n)` (173-179) creates N owners in the same city — the intended driver for any per-city counting rule. The class Javadoc explicitly mentions upcoming "per-city cap" and "most common city" rules, implying a per-city sequence/count is expected.
- Helpers `shaHex`, `luhn` (296-321) exist for other derived-value rules.

No other test files reference `customerCode`, `membership`, `namesake`, or `household`.

---

## 6. Build tool & running tests

- **Maven** (`/home/daniel/sandbox/pom.xml`, wrapper `./mvnw`). No Gradle.
- Run all tests: `./mvnw test`
- Run just the acceptance test: `./mvnw test -Dtest=AcceptanceTest`
- Full build: `./mvnw clean install` (or `mvn` per `readme.md` lines 218/137).
- Note: DTOs are generated from `openapi.yml` during the build, so a clean compile is needed after touching the spec.

---

## Summary of what to edit for the `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` change
1. `CustomerCodeGenerator.generate(...)` — add a city parameter and prepend `<CITY3>` (uppercase first 3 letters of city). File: `service/CustomerCodeGenerator.java`.
2. Update the caller in `ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner` (line 243) to pass `owner.getCity()`. Consider whether `NNNN` should stay global (`ownerRepository.count()`) or become per-city — no per-city count method exists yet, so a per-city sequence would require adding a repository query (all three impls: SpringData, JPA, JDBC).
3. `membershipNumber` needs no change (derives from `customerCode`).
4. Update the `customerCode` description/example in `openapi.yml` (lines 2107-2115) and `CustomerCodeGenerator`'s Javadoc (and Owner's, if referenced).
5. `customer_code` column is VARCHAR(20) — new 12-char code fits, no schema change needed.

